  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The second of the two prayers at the ablutions, said while the priest washes his thumbs and index fingers with wine and water over the chalice. QUEM at w022 is the crux of the prayer: it is masculine accusative singular and therefore cannot agree with the feminine mácula standing beside it, nor with the neuter plural sacraménta that follows. It reaches back over both to ME at w017 — let no stain remain in me, whom the pure and holy sacraments have refreshed. The corpus tags it with its own case, number and gender and leaves the reaching-back to the word's note. The rubric witness do prints before this prayer ends with deinde prosequitur, and the corpus splits it: the ablution belongs to the prayer before, and the two words that hand the priest on belong here, at s01. VISCÉRIBUS at w011 is dative, governed by adhǽreat, which takes the dative as inhærére does at ordinarium.fili-dei-vivi w040.",
    "source": {
      "method": "Verified by collation against the independent witnesses below: zero substantive divergence. The accidental variant is adjudicated in the apparatus.",
      "witnesses": "witnesses/ordinarium.corpus-tuum/",
      "apparatus": "witnesses/ordinarium.corpus-tuum/apparatus.json"
    },
